# Shell scripts must stay LF regardless of platform — CRLF makes bash die with
# `$'\r': command not found` (bit the LAN dry-run + the WSL build env repeatedly).
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
# .sh.example templates get copied to hosts verbatim — same CRLF hazard
# (ktp-scheduled-restart.sh.example sat CRLF in the repo until 2026-07-07).
*.sh.example text eol=lf
# .conf.example files are `source`d by bash after copying — a CRLF value makes
# LAN_IP="" parse as the 1-char value \r and PASS the required-key check
# (lan-deploy.conf.example sat CRLF in the working tree until 2026-07-07).
*.conf.example text eol=lf
# Everything below deploys verbatim to Linux hosts (SFTP copies bytes as-is):
# a fresh Windows clone without these rules materializes CRLF, and a deploy
# then ships \r into systemd unit parsing / python shebangs / ini values.
*.py         text eol=lf
*.py.example text eol=lf
*.service    text eol=lf
*.timer      text eol=lf
*.ini        text eol=lf
*.ini.example text eol=lf
*.json.example text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf
# dodserver.cfg.example is exec'd by the GoldSrc engine after the distributor
# copies it; a CRLF line gives a cvar a value with a trailing \r. Its
# config/online twin is LF, so the two halves of one pair disagreed.
*.cfg.example text eol=lf
*.j2         text eol=lf
*.cron       text eol=lf
*.logrotate       text eol=lf
# cron.d payloads have no extension, so none of the rules above reach them. A
# CRLF crontab makes cron run a command with a trailing backslash-r and fail obscurely.
scripts/cron.d/* text eol=lf
ops/cron-inventory/**/cron.d/* text eol=lf
# sql/ deploys verbatim to a Linux host the same way scripts/ does.
*.sql text eol=lf
# Git hooks have no extension, so none of the rules above reach them. A CRLF
# hook dies on Linux with `$'\r': command not found` -- and a pre-push hook that
# cannot run is a secret gate that never fires.
scripts/hooks/* text eol=lf
# ktp-install is python3 with no extension, so *.py never reaches it — and it is
# the installer that writes DEPLOYED.tsv on live hosts.
scripts/ktp-install text eol=lf
# Workflow `run:` blocks are bash on a Linux runner; CRLF leaks \r into them.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf
# Every other yml here is read on a Linux runner or by docker compose, and the
# workflow rule above reached none of them.
*.yml text eol=lf
# A CRLF `RUN` line reaches the build shell with a trailing \r and fails as a
# missing command, which reads as a broken image rather than a line ending.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
# pip reads this line by line; a trailing \r lands inside the pinned version.
requirements.txt text eol=lf
# A CRLF ignore pattern does not match the path it names, and git says nothing.
.gitignore text eol=lf
# Docs are read on Linux hosts and diffed in review; one of the 339 here was
# MIXED (779 CRLF against 780 LF), which no diff view shows.
*.md text eol=lf
# auditd rule files are parsed by augenrules on the host; a CRLF line makes it
# skip the rule and still exit 0, so the audit reads as "nothing ever wrote there".
*.rules text eol=lf
# GRUB fixtures are parsed by bash/awk in tests; a CRLF line breaks the menu and grubenv parsing.
tests/fixtures/grub_default_kernel/** text eol=lf

# Entries live one file per change in changelog.d/ precisely because every PR
# used to insert its section at the same offset under `## [Unreleased]`, which
# conflicted every sibling on this file alone and re-blocked the queue after
# each merge.
# `union` keeps BOTH sides of a conflicting hunk — the resolution applied by
# hand every time — but GitHub does not run merge drivers server-side, so it was
# never the fix. It is kept for local merges and for the hazard it documents.
# It is the correct driver only because this file is append-only: never set it
# on a file where one side's edit is meant to REPLACE the other's.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union

# Overview descriptors are copied to the fleet byte-for-byte and three of the
# seven here are CRLF as their mapper wrote them. core.autocrlf=input rewrites
# CRLF on commit, so without this the repo would hold a file nobody authored and
# the deploy would ship it.
config/overviews/*.txt -text

# The AC stock path list is read line-by-line by build-game-files-manifest.py on
# Linux and on a Windows clone; pin LF so both sides parse identical bytes.
scripts/data/*.txt text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf
